1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,951
Some work does not belong in a web request, like cleaning a cache

2
00:00:04,951 --> 00:00:07,065
or publishing scheduled content.

3
00:00:07,065 --> 00:00:09,736
Orchard Core runs it as a background task.

4
00:00:09,736 --> 00:00:13,742
In this video, you write one, schedule it, make it safe on several

5
00:00:13,742 --> 00:00:16,190
servers, and read its state from code.

6
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:24,157
One background service runs for the whole application.

7
00:00:24,157 --> 00:00:28,478
On every round, about a minute apart by default, it visits each running

8
00:00:28,478 --> 00:00:33,248
tenant, that is, each site of the application, and runs the tasks that are due.

9
00:00:33,248 --> 00:00:36,390
A tenant's tasks are those its modules registered.

10
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:40,180
Each run gets its own service scope from the tenant.

11
00:00:40,180 --> 00:00:43,954
The tasks of a tenant run one after another, and the service waits

12
00:00:43,954 --> 00:00:47,990
for all of them before its next round, so a task should finish quickly.

13
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:52,435
The Background Tasks feature is not needed for any of this.

14
00:00:52,435 --> 00:00:57,266
It adds the admin pages, and it stores the settings that someone changes there.

15
00:00:57,266 --> 00:01:00,620
Without it, a task uses the values you wrote in code.

16
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:10,360
A task is a class that implements IBackgroundTask, which has one method, DoWorkAsync.

17
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:15,230
It receives the service provider of the run, and a cancellation token that

18
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:17,970
is canceled when the application stops.

19
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:24,000
Orchard Core registers its tasks as singletons, so a constructor takes services that

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,344
live at least as long as the tenant, like a logger.

21
00:01:27,344 --> 00:01:31,920
Services tied to the run, like the session, are taken from the provider.

22
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:37,315
If DoWorkAsync throws, the service logs the error, records the run as failed

23
00:01:37,315 --> 00:01:40,431
with its message, and goes on with the next task.

24
00:01:40,431 --> 00:01:44,397
This task catches and logs the errors of its cleanup, so they do

25
00:01:44,397 --> 00:01:45,700
not fail the run.

26
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:53,046
The BackgroundTask attribute describes the task.

27
00:01:53,046 --> 00:01:56,134
Title and Description are what the admin shows.

28
00:01:56,134 --> 00:02:00,270
Without a Title, the admin builds one from the name of the class,

29
00:02:00,270 --> 00:02:04,640
so ResizedMediaCacheBackgroundTask would show as Resized Media Cache.

30
00:02:05,390 --> 00:02:09,468
Schedule is a cron expression, read in the time zone of the site.

31
00:02:09,468 --> 00:02:13,833
In the attribute it defaults to every five minutes, and a task without

32
00:02:13,833 --> 00:02:16,475
the attribute is scheduled every minute.

33
00:02:16,475 --> 00:02:18,830
A task runs at most once per round.

34
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:24,413
A schedule that is not a valid cron expression never starts a scheduled run.

35
00:02:24,413 --> 00:02:28,951
Enable set to false stops the scheduled runs, as on the content version

36
00:02:28,951 --> 00:02:32,370
pruning task, but a task can still be run on demand.

37
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:38,175
UsePipeline builds the tenant pipeline before the run, so a task can generate URLs.

38
00:02:38,175 --> 00:02:43,060
Settings saved in the admin replace the values of the attribute for that tenant.

39
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:52,982
Register the task in the Startup class of your module, with AddSingleton, as IBackgroundTask.

40
00:02:52,982 --> 00:02:58,010
It then runs for every tenant where the feature of that Startup class is enabled.

41
00:03:02,217 --> 00:03:09,054
With the Background Tasks feature enabled, the Resized Media Cache Cleaner appears under Tools, Background Tasks.

42
00:03:09,054 --> 00:03:13,593
Its title and description come from the attribute, and the Next run badge

43
00:03:13,593 --> 00:03:15,317
comes from its schedule.

44
00:03:15,776 --> 00:03:17,092
Let's open the task.

45
00:03:17,092 --> 00:03:20,889
The Settings card shows its technical name, which is the full name of

46
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:24,636
the class, and its schedule, the cron expression from the attribute.

47
00:03:25,095 --> 00:03:29,604
Let's open Actions and queue a run on this server, whatever the schedule.

48
00:03:29,604 --> 00:03:32,514
The Execution card then reports the last run:

49
00:03:32,514 --> 00:03:35,825
its result, how long it took, and what triggered it.

50
00:03:40,025 --> 00:03:45,844
When an application runs on several servers, each server runs its own background service.

51
00:03:45,844 --> 00:03:51,235
A scheduled task then runs on every server, and by default these runs can overlap.

52
00:03:51,985 --> 00:03:58,833
Set LockTimeout and LockExpiration, in milliseconds and both above zero, and the task is atomic.

53
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:04,205
Before each run, the service tries to acquire a lock named after the task.

54
00:04:04,205 --> 00:04:08,905
If it cannot in time, the service tries again on the next round.

55
00:04:09,655 --> 00:04:13,159
A lock needs a distributed lock feature, such as Redis Lock.

56
00:04:13,159 --> 00:04:15,602
Without one, these settings are ignored.

57
00:04:15,602 --> 00:04:19,372
Set the expiration above the longest run of the task, or the lock

58
00:04:19,372 --> 00:04:21,655
can expire while the task is running.

59
00:04:25,855 --> 00:04:31,160
The service reads the BackgroundService section of the OrchardCore configuration.

60
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:36,893
PollingTime and MinimumIdleTime set its pace, MaxRecentRuns sets how many runs each task

61
00:04:36,893 --> 00:04:42,381
remembers, and ShellWarmup loads every tenant at startup, so their tasks run without

62
00:04:42,381 --> 00:04:44,455
waiting for a first request.

63
00:04:48,655 --> 00:04:55,039
To react to every run of every task of a tenant, implement IBackgroundTaskEventHandler.

64
00:04:55,039 --> 00:05:01,424
ExecutingAsync is called before a task starts, and ExecutedAsync after it ends, whether

65
00:05:01,424 --> 00:05:03,415
it succeeded or failed.

66
00:05:04,165 --> 00:05:08,476
The context carries the name of the task, the tenant, the services of

67
00:05:08,476 --> 00:05:11,523
the scope, and the exception when there is one.

68
00:05:11,523 --> 00:05:13,823
Register the handler as a service.

69
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:18,135
An error thrown by a handler is logged, and the task is not affected.

70
00:05:22,335 --> 00:05:26,845
IBackgroundTaskMonitor gives your code the execution state of the tasks.

71
00:05:26,845 --> 00:05:31,528
It is registered with the background service, so inject an enumerable of it

72
00:05:31,528 --> 00:05:35,345
if your code may run in an application without that service.

73
00:05:36,095 --> 00:05:40,457
GetStateAsync takes the tenant name and the task name, which is the full

74
00:05:40,457 --> 00:05:41,799
name of its class.

75
00:05:41,799 --> 00:05:46,049
The state holds the status, the run and failure counts, the next start

76
00:05:46,049 --> 00:05:49,125
time, and the last run with its result and error.

77
00:05:49,875 --> 00:05:55,634
RequestRunAsync queues a run on this server, whatever the schedule, and returns Queued,

78
00:05:55,634 --> 00:05:58,607
AlreadyQueued, AlreadyRunning or NotFound.

79
00:05:58,607 --> 00:06:04,615
The state lives in memory on each server, so it starts again when the application restarts.

80
00:06:06,615 --> 00:06:08,523
That is a background task:

81
00:06:08,523 --> 00:06:13,176
a class, an attribute and one registration, with locks for web farms and

82
00:06:13,176 --> 00:06:15,144
a monitor to read its runs.

83
00:06:15,144 --> 00:06:16,695
Thanks for watching.

